    "terminalReason": "completed",
    "text": "I made all three improvements in the image. All 14 tests pass, both before and after the changes.\n\n**1. Duplicated code in tests 2 and 3:** Both tests now use a new helper, `assertOnlySaleMadeTo:is:`. I also moved `salesSystem := CoffeeShopSalesSystem new` into `setUp`, since every test was repeating it.\n\n**2. Duplicated code in the last 6 tests:** The shared steps (record the purchases, get the reward, check quantity, product and price) are now in `assertRewardOf:afterBuying:is:of:at:`. Two shorter helpers call it: `assertRule1RewardOf:isAt:` and `assertRule2RewardOf:isAt:`. Each of tests 9\u201314 is now a single line with the customer and the expected price.\n\n**3. `if`s replaced by polymorphism:**\n- **Products:** `CoffeeShopProduct` now has two subclasses, `CoffeeShopCombo1` and `CoffeeShopCombo2`, and each one knows its own `price` and `volume`. So I removed `CoffeeShopSalesSystem>>volumeOf:` and `RewardRule>>priceOf:`. Two combos of the same kind count as equal, which the sales tests need. The test methods `combo1` and `combo2` now return these objects.\n- **Customer types:** Gold, Silver and Normal are now subclasses of `CoffeeShopCustomerType`. Each one answers its own reward prices: a fixed price for Combo1 and a percentage of the price for Combo2. You still create customers with `named: ... ofType: #Gold`: the right type class is looked up by the symbol, the same way `RewardRule for:ifNone:` already picks a rule.\n- **Reward rules:** `createRewardFor:` is now written once, in `RewardRule`. Each subclass only says which product it rewards and asks the customer for the price. I also removed the `product` instance variable it used to set, which nothing needed.\n\nEverything else is in `IngSof1-2019-2C-1Parcial`. `CoffeeShopTest` was in that category too, so I moved it to `IngSof1-2019-2C-1Parcial-Tests`."
